    ))}
  </div>

  <p>
    That overlap is exactly why people move between the two fields more often than most career comparisons
    suggest — the core research and editing craft transfers, and only the specialised layer (SEO structuring on
    one side, persuasion compression on the other) needs to be built fresh.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ai-impact">How AI is squeezing content writing and copywriting, differently</h2>

  <p>
    A common mistake in this comparison is treating "AI can write now" as one uniform threat. It is not — the
    pressure lands very differently on each field's easiest layer.
  </p>

  <p>
    In content writing, a 2026 survey of more than 1,100 content firms, freelance writers, and content
    outsourcing companies named generative AI the most disruptive technological shift the content-writing
    industry has faced in its history. Routine, keyword-driven SEO article writing is declining in demand,
    pricing has come under real downward pressure, and clients now scrutinise human-written work more closely
    than before. At the same time, entirely new roles — AI content strategist, AI editor, prompt engineer,
    content ethicist — are emerging for writers who invest in AI-fluent skills rather than resisting the tools.
  </p>

  <p>
    In copywriting, AI-assisted tools are just as widely used, and India's job market already lists hundreds of
    roles explicitly combining AI and copywriting skills. But the work that survives AI pressure best in
    copywriting is strategy-heavy, brand-voice, and conversion-tested campaign work — the parts that depend on
    reading a specific audience's psychology, not just producing grammatically correct persuasive sentences.
    The templated layer — generic ad variants, routine product descriptions, basic subject lines — faces the
    same commoditisation pressure content writing's bottom layer does.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      In both fields, AI is shrinking the "generic, templated" layer and growing the value of the person who
      owns strategy, brand judgment, niche depth, or a tested result an AI tool cannot claim credit for.
      Efficient writers in both fields now use AI as a drafting accelerant, then spend their real skill on
      editing, fact-checking, and infusing the piece with a genuine human angle or tested persuasive judgment.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    Rather than asking "which field is safer from AI," a sharper question is: in whichever lane you pick, are
    you building the strategic, niche, or persuasion-judgment layer AI still cannot do well, or are you doing
    the routine task AI is about to do faster and cheaper than you?
  </p>

