  <h2 id="gates">Pass The 3 Gates before you spend months on this</h2>

  <p>
    The 4-Checkpoint Protocol tells you which lane fits on paper. The 3 Gates make you test it in the real
    world before you commit months of unpaid portfolio-building or a niche pivot to it.
  </p>

  <div class="gates-block">
    <p class="gates-block__title">
      Do not treat either lane as decided before passing all three gates in your chosen path.
    </p>
    <div class="gates-row">
      <div class="gate">
        <span class="gate__num">Gate 1</span>
        <span class="gate__name">Proof of skill</span>
        <p>
          For content writing, write one genuinely researched, 1,500+ word piece on a real topic in a niche you
          are considering, not a generic "5 tips" filler post. For copywriting, write one full concept — a
          headline, three variations, and a short landing page — for a real or fictional product, built to sell,
          not just to sound clever.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="gate">
        <span class="gate__num">Gate 2</span>
        <span class="gate__name">Proof of communication</span>
        <p>
          For content writing, explain in under two minutes why your piece serves the reader's real question,
          not just why it reads well. For copywriting, pitch your headline and explain the specific psychological
          reason it should convert, in the same short window a creative director would actually give you.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="gate">
        <span class="gate__num">Gate 3</span>
        <span class="gate__name">Proof of value</span>
        <p>
          Show the work to a working content writer or a working copywriter and ask one direct question: "Would
          this get published, paid for, or shipped at your company?" Use their answer, not your own hope, to
          decide.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="switching-later">Can you switch between them later?</h2>

  <p>
    Yes, and the switch happens in both directions more often than most career guides admit.
  </p>

  <p>
    Content writers moving into copywriting usually need to build a concept-driven portfolio of short,
    persuasive work, since agencies hire on creative compression and craft, not on long-form samples. Their
    research discipline and editing instinct are a real head start; what they are missing is the persuasion
    muscle and the comfort with rapid, high-rejection iteration.
  </p>

  <p>
    Copywriters moving into content writing usually need to build SEO fluency and the patience for longer
    research cycles, since copywriting trains fast, compressed thinking rather than sustained, in-depth
    explanation. Their persuasion instincts transfer well and often make them sharper, more engaging content
    writers than people who never had that discipline.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--amber">
    <p>
      Neither switch is instant, and neither switch is rare. Treat your first choice as a strong starting
      lane, not a life sentence — the skill overlap between content writing and copywriting is bigger than
      most comparison charts suggest.
    </p>
  </div>

